      "text": "To find the area of the parallelogram generated by the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$, we first need to determine if these vectors are orthogonal (perpendicular) to each other. If they are orthogonal, the area of the parallelogram is simply given by the magnitude of their cross product.\n\nThe cross product $\\mathbf{v_1} \\times \\mathbf{v_2}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v_1} \\times \\mathbf{v_2} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n3 & 1 & -2 \\\\\n1 & -3 & 4\n\\end{vmatrix}\n= \\mathbf{i}(1 \\cdot 4 - (-2) \\cdot (-3)) - \\mathbf{j}(3 \\cdot 4 - (-2) \\cdot 1) + \\mathbf{k}(3 \\cdot (-3) - 1 \\cdot 1)\n= \\mathbf{i}(4 - 6) - \\mathbf{j}(12 + 2) + \\mathbf{k}(-9 - 1)\n= -2\\mathbf{i} - 14\\mathbf{j} - 10\\mathbf{k}\n= \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of this cross product vector is:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-14)^2 + (-10)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 196 + 100} = \\sqrt{300} = 10\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nSo the area of the parallelogram generated by the given vectors is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this calculation with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the vectors\nv1 = np.array([3, 1, -2])\nv2 = np.array([1, -3, 4])\n\n# Compute the cross product\ncross_product = np.cross(v1, v2)\n\n# Compute the magnitude of the cross product\narea = np.linalg.norm(cross_product)\nprint(area)\n```",
